From: Arcadia maintainers <maintainers@retrofactory.net>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/rockchip: defer legacy gamma programming while VOP2 is off

The legacy gamma callback can be invoked before the display controller is
powered up, for example when Plymouth initializes a CRTC before its first
modeset. vop2_crtc_load_lut() already avoids register access in that case,
but the callback then unconditionally calls vop2_cfg_done(), which accesses
VOP2 registers and can hang the RK3566.

Keep the requested LUT in vp->lut and mark it active. The CRTC enable path
already restores an active LUT once the controller is enabled. Avoid both
cfg_done and the subsequent standby register read until that point. Preserve
the existing behavior for calls while the controller is enabled.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [Rockchip vendor kernel]

---
 drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/rockchip_drm_vop2.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/rockchip_drm_vop2.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/rockchip_drm_vop2.c
@@ -4189,6 +4189,16 @@ static int vop2_crtc_legacy_gamma_set(struct drm_crtc *crtc, u16 *red,
 	for (i = 0; i < size; i++)
 		rockchip_vop2_crtc_fb_gamma_set(crtc, red[i], green[i],
 						blue[i], i);
+	/*
+	 * The LUT is stored in memory even if the controller has not been
+	 * powered up. Its registers cannot be accessed until it is enabled.
+	 * The CRTC enable path restores an active LUT after enabling VOP2.
+	 */
+	if (!vop2->is_enabled) {
+		vp->gamma_lut_active = true;
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	vop2_crtc_load_lut(crtc);
 	vop2_cfg_done(crtc);
 	/*
